<section id="first-steps">
    <h2>First steps after the installation</h2>
    <p class="guide-meta">Who: Admin · Time: 15 minutes · Done once</p>
    <p><b>Why?</b> After the installation no server, user or cloud connection is defined. These steps are the base of every other scenario, and the order matters:
       each step builds on the previous one.</p>
    <h3>Steps</h3>
    <ol>
        <li><b>Set the administrator password.</b> On the server itself open <code>https://localhost</code>, click <b>Local administrator</b> and choose a new password.
            <GuideFigure Src="guide/login.png" Caption="Sign-in: corporate users sign in with Windows (AD); the local administrator is for the first setup." />
        </li>
        <li><b>Tell where the files go.</b> In <b>Administration → Destinations</b> click <b>+ New destination</b>, choose the provider – <b>Google Cloud Storage</b>,
            <b>Amazon S3 / S3-compatible</b> (MinIO, Wasabi, Ceph …) or <b>Azure Blob Storage</b> – enter the bucket or container and the key, then click <b>Test</b>.
            <GuideFigure Src="guide/destinations.png" Caption="Destinations: one row per bucket or container; Test writes a small test object with the stored key." />
            For GCS you can instead upload the service account key once in <b>Settings → Google Cloud Storage credential</b> and use it for every GCS destination.
            <GuideFigure Src="guide/settings-gcs.png" Caption="GCS key on the Settings page: stored encrypted; only the service account's name is shown." />
        </li>
        <li><b>Import the corporate HTTPS certificate</b> (<b>Settings → HTTPS certificate</b>). Browsers warn about the temporary certificate of the installation.</li>
        <li><b>Add the servers</b> (<b>Servers</b>): the source SQL Servers (read from) and the target SQL Server (DWH); click <b>Test</b> for each.</li>
        <li><b>Check permissions:</b> <b>Check permissions</b> on the <b>Servers</b> page compares the service accounts' rights with what they need.</li>
        <li><b>Give people roles</b> (see <a href="/guide#access">Give people access</a>).</li>
    </ol>
    <div class="alert alert-info"><b>Recommended.</b> Give roles to AD groups (e.g. <i>DWH-Editors</i>) rather than to individuals: access follows group membership automatically.</div>
    <div class="alert alert-warning"><b>Watch out.</b> The local administrator is for the first setup and emergencies only; keep its password safe. Everyday work uses Windows accounts.</div>
    <p><b>How do I check?</b> The <b>Status</b> page shows at least one worker online, and the permission check has no red (<i>Missing</i>) rows.</p>
    <p class="small">Reference: <a href="/help#installation">Installation</a> · <a href="/help#https">HTTPS certificate</a> · <a href="/help#destinations">Export destinations</a> · <a href="/help#servers">Servers and service accounts</a> · <a href="/help#permission-check">Permission check</a></p>
</section>

<section id="nightly-table">
    <h2>Copy a table to the DWH every night</h2>
    <p class="guide-meta">Who: Editor · Time: 5 minutes</p>
    <p><b>Why?</b> Reports and analyses run on the copy in the DWH instead of loading the operational systems (ERP, CRM). A nightly copy can be queried fast and safely during the day.</p>
    <p><b>Before you start:</b> the source and target servers exist on <b>Servers</b> and you are permitted to use the databases.</p>
    <h3>Steps</h3>
    <ol>
        <li><b>Extract Jobs → + New job</b>. Choose <b>Source server</b> and <b>Source database</b> and tick the table; the columns are selected automatically.
            <b>Preview</b> shows the first 50 rows.
            <GuideFigure Src="guide/extract-source.png" Caption="1 · Source: server, database and table. 'Read a consistent snapshot' is recommended for busy sources." />
        </li>
        <li><b>Choose the target.</b> Pick <b>Target server</b> and <b>Target database</b>. The schema is filled from the source name (ERP → <code>erp</code>) and the table keeps the source name.
            <b>Check target</b> shows differences if the table exists; otherwise it is created on the first run.</li>
        <li><b>Choose the load mode</b> – the main decision:
            <ul>
                <li>Only the <b>current state</b> is needed: <b>Replace</b> for small tables, <b>Merge</b> for large ones.</li>
                <li>A <b>snapshot of every day</b> (e.g. stock levels): <b>Append</b> with a <b>Retention</b>.</li>
            </ul>
            <GuideFigure Src="guide/extract-target.png" Caption="2 · Target and 3 · Load. Replace has no retention: the table always holds the latest load." />
        </li>
        <li><b>Schedule it.</b> In <b>4 · Trigger</b> keep <b>On a schedule</b> and pick "Every night at 02:00" under <b>Preset</b>. The form shows the next 5 runs.
            <GuideFigure Src="guide/extract-trigger.png" Caption="4 · Trigger: the schedule. Addresses in Notify hear about this job's problems." />
        </li>
        <li><b>Save and run now</b> saves the job and runs it once right away.</li>
    </ol>
    <div class="alert alert-info"><b>Recommended.</b> Spread nightly jobs over time instead of starting all at 02:00; the source server will thank you. For jobs that depend on each other use a <a href="/guide#nightly-export">dependency</a> instead of a time.</div>
    <div class="alert alert-warning"><b>Watch out.</b> Do not write same-named tables of different sources (<i>Customers</i> in ERP and CRM) to one target schema – the schema taken from the source name prevents it.
        The <i>Strict</i> policy refuses to write into an existing table with a different structure; that is a safety net.</div>
    <p><b>How do I check?</b> The job is <i>Succeeded</i> in <b>Run history</b> and the row count matches your expectation.</p>
    <p class="small">Reference: <a href="/help#extract-source">Choosing the source</a> · <a href="/help#extract-target">Target table and schema policy</a> · <a href="/help#load-modes">Load modes</a> · <a href="/help#retention">Retention</a> · <a href="/help#cron">Scheduling</a></p>
</section>

<section id="large-tables">
    <h2>Large, constantly changing tables</h2>
    <p class="guide-meta">Who: Editor · Example: order or customer tables with millions of rows</p>
    <p><b>Why?</b> Reading a large table completely every night loads the source and inflates the files. With the right mode only changed rows are read
       and only the changes go to the cloud (25 rows when 25 of 5,000 customers changed).</p>
    <h3>Which mode?</h3>
    <table class="table table-sm table-bordered bg-white">
        <thead class="table-light"><tr><th>Need</th><th>Mode</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
            <tr><td><b>Current state</b> of every record, with a modification time column</td><td><b>Merge</b> + watermark (<code>ModifiedAt</code>)</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Current state, no modification column, deleted rows must disappear too</td><td><b>Merge</b>, full read + "Delete rows whose key the source no longer returns"</td></tr>
            <tr><td>The <b>history</b> of every change (a record several times)</td><td><b>Incremental</b> + watermark</td></tr>
        </tbody>
    </table>
    <h3>Steps (Merge)</h3>
    <ol>
        <li>In <b>3 · Load</b> choose <b>Merge</b> as <b>Load mode</b>. The source table's primary key is ticked under <b>Key columns</b>.</li>
        <li>If there is a modification time column, choose it as <b>Watermark column</b>: each night only rows changed since the last read are read.</li>
        <li>If rows are physically deleted in the source and must be deleted in the target too, leave the watermark empty and tick "Delete rows whose key the source no longer returns".
            <GuideFigure Src="guide/merge-load.png" Caption="Merge: the key (PK) is suggested; unchanged rows are not touched." />
        </li>
        <li>For busy sources enable <b>Read a consistent snapshot</b> in <b>1 · Source</b> (the DBA allows it once; the form shows the state).</li>
    </ol>
    <div class="alert alert-info"><b>Recommended.</b> A <code>rowversion</code> column is the best watermark: it does not miss transactions committed late. A modification time column is fine in most cases.</div>
    <div class="alert alert-warning"><b>Watch out.</b> The key must identify one row. If the source returns a key twice, the job stops and leaves the target unchanged – on purpose.
        <b>WITH (NOLOCK)</b> can skip or double rows on large busy tables; use the snapshot option instead.</div>
    <p><b>How do I check?</b> Merge jobs show <code>merge: +inserted ~updated -deleted</code> in <b>Run history</b>.</p>
    <p class="small">Reference: <a href="/help#merge">Merge</a> · <a href="/help#load-modes">Load modes</a> · <a href="/help#consistency">Integrity and consistent reads</a></p>
</section>

<section id="nightly-export">
    <h2>Send the collected data to the cloud every night</h2>
    <p class="guide-meta">Who: Editor · Time: 5 minutes</p>
    <p><b>Why?</b> Analytics teams in the cloud (BigQuery, Databricks) expect the data every day, regularly and complete. One "All collected tables" export covers every collected
       table of the DWH and picks up new tables automatically.</p>
    <h3>Steps</h3>
    <ol>
        <li><b>Exports → + New export</b>. Choose the DWH server and database and the mode <b>All collected tables</b>.</li>
        <li>In <b>2 · Destination</b> choose where the files go (GCS, S3 or Azure). If the list is empty, an Admin adds one on <b>Destinations</b> first.
            The object name template can stay as it is: files are sorted into table and day folders.</li>
        <li><b>Choose the file format:</b> <b>CSV</b> for Excel and general use, <b>Parquet</b> for BigQuery, Spark and pandas (about 7× smaller, typed).
            <GuideFigure Src="guide/export-format.png" Caption="3 · File format: CSV settings (delimiter, quoting, NULL) or Parquet compression." />
        </li>
        <li><b>Tie the schedule to the extracts:</b> in <b>4 · Trigger</b> choose <b>After other jobs</b> and tick the extract jobs.
            The export starts by itself when all of them have succeeded.
            <GuideFigure Src="guide/export-trigger.png" Caption="After other jobs: no guessing of times; the export starts when the extracts are done and waits if one fails." />
        </li>
        <li><b>Save</b>. Try it with <b>Run now</b> on the job page.</li>
    </ol>
    <div class="alert alert-info"><b>Recommended.</b> Add a <i>lifecycle</i> rule to the bucket or container (e.g. 90 days) to remove old files; SqlHarbor never deletes files in the cloud.</div>
    <div class="alert alert-warning"><b>Watch out.</b> Read the <a href="/guide#personal-data">personal data</a> scenario before sending. "All collected tables" also sends every newly added table automatically.</div>
    <p><b>How do I check?</b> The export row in <b>Run history</b> shows tables, rows, files and the addresses of the files (<code>gs://</code>, <code>s3://</code> or <code>https://…blob.core.windows.net</code>).</p>
    <p class="small">Reference: <a href="/help#export-modes">Export modes</a> · <a href="/help#csv-format">CSV and Parquet</a> · <a href="/help#destinations">Export destinations</a> · <a href="/help#gcs">Object names</a> · <a href="/help#dependencies">Job dependencies</a></p>
</section>

<section id="personal-data">
    <h2>Keep personal data out of the cloud</h2>
    <p class="guide-meta">Who: Admin · Recommended for GDPR/KVKK</p>
    <p><b>Why?</b> E-mail addresses, national IDs, IBANs and phone numbers may stay in the DWH but must not be readable in the cloud files. SqlHarbor masks them while writing the files
       and notices a forgotten rule before anything is sent.</p>
    <h3>Steps</h3>
    <ol>
        <li>On <b>Data protection</b> choose the DWH server and database and click <b>Scan</b>. Columns with personal data are listed, with partly hidden examples.</li>
        <li><b>Create rule</b> on each row creates a rule. Suggested methods: e-mail → <b>Email</b>, national ID → <b>Hash</b>, IBAN and card → <b>Partial</b>.
            A rule can be broader than one table: column pattern <code>*Email*</code>, scope <code>*</code>.</li>
        <li>Keep the <b>Export guard</b> on <b>Warn</b> for the first weeks; switch to <b>Block</b> once the warnings are gone.
            <GuideFigure Src="guide/privacy.png" Caption="Data protection: export guard, masking rules and the personal data scan." />
        </li>
    </ol>
    <div class="alert alert-info"><b>Recommended.</b> Use <b>Hash</b> when analysts need to match customers across tables: the same value becomes the same code everywhere, but it cannot be reversed.</div>
    <div class="alert alert-warning"><b>Watch out.</b> Rules match column names. Free SQL can rename a column (<code>SELECT Email AS X</code>) – the export guard catches that.
        "Already when collecting" writes masked values into the DWH too; it cannot be undone, choose it deliberately.</div>
    <p><b>How do I check?</b> Scan again: every column shows a rule under "Masked by", and exports show no "Data protection warning".</p>
    <p class="small">Reference: <a href="/help#masking">Data protection (masking)</a></p>
</section>

<section id="trust-the-data">
    <h2>Make sure the data is complete and correct</h2>
    <p class="guide-meta">Who: Editor · Recommended for critical tables</p>
    <p><b>Why?</b> A "succeeded" job does not mean the data is right: the source system's nightly batch may have stopped halfway, or a filter may be wrong.
       Checks catch that and keep bad data from reaching the cloud.</p>
    <h3>Automatic (nothing to set up)</h3>
    <ul>
        <li>Every load: rows read from the source = rows written to the target. Every export: rows read from the DWH = rows in the files. A difference fails the run.</li>
    </ul>
    <h3>What you add</h3>
    <ol>
        <li>In job section <b>5 · Data quality</b> click <b>+ Add check</b>. Give every critical table at least
            <b>Minimum rows per load</b> (no empty loads) and <b>Row count matches the source</b> (same count as the source; on changing sources set an increasing ID as <i>cutoff column</i>).</li>
        <li>Add <b>Duplicate keys</b> for key columns and <b>Custom SQL</b> for business rules (e.g. no negative amounts).</li>
        <li>Choose the severity: <b>Warning</b> only warns; <b>Error</b> also holds back the export that runs after this job.</li>
        <li>Enter hours in "Alert when there is no good load for (hours)" (e.g. 26): you are told when the data is older than a day.
            <GuideFigure Src="guide/extract-quality.png" Caption="5 · Data quality: the checks and their latest results." />
        </li>
    </ol>
    <GuideFigure Src="guide/quality-page.png" Caption="The Data quality page: the latest state of every job's checks and stale jobs." />
    <div class="alert alert-info"><b>Recommended.</b> Start with <b>Warning</b>, watch the results for a few days, tune the thresholds, then make the critical ones <b>Error</b> – no alert fatigue.</div>
    <div class="alert alert-warning"><b>Watch out.</b> Checks do not fix or undo data; they report and hold back dependent jobs. Fix the cause in the source and run the job again.</div>
    <p class="small">Reference: <a href="/help#quality">Data quality checks</a> · <a href="/help#consistency">Integrity and consistent reads</a></p>
</section>

<section id="get-alerted">
    <h2>Get told when something goes wrong</h2>
    <p class="guide-meta">Who: Admin (channels), Editor (a job's Notify field)</p>
    <p><b>Why?</b> A nightly failure should not be discovered when someone opens a report in the morning. Alerts arrive the minute it happens.</p>
    <h3>Steps</h3>
    <ol>
        <li>On <b>Alerts</b> click <b>+ New channel</b>: e-mail addresses, a Microsoft Teams channel (Workflows webhook) or a generic webhook.</li>
        <li>Choose the events of the channel. Recommended for a team channel: <i>Failed, Missed, QualityFailed, Stale, NoServer</i>.</li>
        <li>Try it with <b>Send test</b>.</li>
        <li>For job owners put their e-mail addresses into the job's <b>Notify</b> field; they get only their own jobs' problems.
            <GuideFigure Src="guide/alerts.png" Caption="Alerts: repeat interval, channels and recent deliveries." />
        </li>
    </ol>
    <div class="alert alert-info"><b>Recommended.</b> The same problem is not repeated within 60 minutes, and a "Recovered" message follows when it is fixed. A Teams channel is the easiest for teams with on-call duty.</div>
    <div class="alert alert-warning"><b>Watch out.</b> A completely stopped application cannot send alerts. Add <code>/health</code> to your monitoring system.</div>
    <p class="small">Reference: <a href="/help#alerts">Alerts</a></p>
</section>

<section id="access">
    <h2>Give people access</h2>
    <p class="guide-meta">Who: Admin</p>
    <p><b>Why?</b> Not everyone should pull data from every database. Roles decide <i>what</i> someone can do, server and database permissions decide <i>where</i>.</p>
    <h3>Steps</h3>
    <ol>
        <li>On <b>Permissions</b> give an AD group a role: <b>Viewer</b> (sees), <b>Editor</b> (creates and runs jobs), <b>Admin</b> (everything).</li>
        <li>For Editors add which servers and databases they may use as <b>source</b> or <b>target</b>. Without a database the permission covers every database of the server.</li>
        <li>Changes reach signed-in users within 10 minutes.</li>
    </ol>
    <div class="alert alert-info"><b>Recommended.</b> Grant roles and permissions to AD groups, not to people; joiners and leavers are then handled in AD.</div>
    <div class="alert alert-warning"><b>Watch out.</b> Editors can run free SQL on sources. The real security boundary is the service account's rights: grant read-only on sources
        (<b>Check permissions</b> shows more as <i>Excess</i>).</div>
    <p class="small">Reference: <a href="/help#roles">Roles and permissions</a> · <a href="/help#permission-check">Permission check</a></p>
</section>

<section id="when-it-fails">
    <h2>A job failed: what now?</h2>
    <p class="guide-meta">Who: Editor, Admin</p>
    <p><b>Why it matters:</b> a failed job leaves no half data behind (its rows are rolled back), but exports waiting for it stop too. Finding the reason quickly saves the day's data.</p>
    <h3>Steps</h3>
    <ol>
        <li>Look at <b>Status</b>: "Last run with problems" and the error column are the first clue. If "Workers online" is 0, the service is stopped – start it first.
            <GuideFigure Src="guide/status-overview.png" Caption="Status: workers online, active jobs and the outcome of the latest runs." />
        </li>
        <li>Open the failed row in <b>Run history</b> for the message and details; the job name links to the job.
            <GuideFigure Src="guide/runs.png" Caption="Run history: status, duration, rows and details of every run." />
        </li>
        <li>By message: <i>Strict schema policy</i> → <b>Check target</b>; <i>Login failed</i> → the service account's rights; <i>Snapshot reads are not allowed</i> → the DBA's setting;
            <i>Integrity check failed</i> → run again and tell IT if it repeats.</li>
        <li>After the fix click <b>Run now</b> on the job page. Exports that run after this job continue by themselves once it succeeds.</li>
    </ol>
    <div class="alert alert-info"><b>Recommended.</b> Hangfire retries a failure automatically (extract 2×, export 3×). Short network problems usually resolve themselves.</div>
    <p class="small">Reference: <a href="/help#troubleshooting">Statuses and troubleshooting</a> · <a href="/help#hangfire">Hangfire dashboard</a></p>
</section>
